    "text": "<strong> Paragraph: </strong> A fixed mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></math> of a gas is subjected to transformation of states from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> and back to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> as shown in the figure<br /><br><br /><br><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0025_9c36b9ef744f.jpg\" style=\"width: 248px; height: 206px;\" /><br> <strong> Question : </strong>The succeeding operations that enable this transformation of states are"
